# 02 — Install Dataverse in Docker

This brings up a local Harvard Dataverse instance using the **official upstream
container stack** (the "Demo or Evaluation" setup). The commands below are
verified against Dataverse 6.10.x; if the upstream guide has moved on, cross-check
the current [Container Guide → Demo or Evaluation](https://guides.dataverse.org/en/latest/container/running/demo.html).

## Step 1 — Get the compose file

The stack is driven by a single `compose.yml` published by the Dataverse
project. Download it from the upstream guide (the **"Quickstart"** section links
the current `compose.yml`):

> <https://guides.dataverse.org/en/latest/container/running/demo.html>

Put it in a working directory of its own, e.g.:

```bash
mkdir dataverse-demo && cd dataverse-demo
# place the downloaded compose.yml here
```

> Keep this directory: the EDMB setup script (step 03) expects to be run from
> here, because it touches `./docker-dev-volumes/solr/...`.

## Step 2 — First run (quickstart)

```bash
docker compose up
```

This pulls the images (`gdcc/dataverse:latest`, `gdcc/configbaker:latest`,
PostgreSQL, Solr, an SMTP stub) and bootstraps the installation. Watch the logs;
the **`bootstrap`** container runs once and then exits — that's expected and
signals setup is complete.

Then visit **<http://localhost:8080>** and log in:

- username: `dataverseAdmin`
- password: `admin1`

If you can log in, the install works. Verify the version any time at
<http://localhost:8080/api/info/version>.

### Stopping / starting / resetting

- **Stop:** `Ctrl-C` in the terminal running compose (data persists in `./data`).
- **Start again:** `docker compose up`.
- **Start fresh (wipe DB):** stop the containers, delete the `data` directory,
  then `docker compose up` again.
- **Tear down completely:** `docker compose down` (optionally also remove `data`
  and `docker-dev-volumes`).

## Step 3 — (Recommended) switch to the "demo" persona

The default `dev` persona bootstraps with admin APIs wide open (`--insecure`).
For anything beyond a throwaway test, switch to the more secure `demo` persona.
In `compose.yml`, find the `bootstrap` service:

```yaml
bootstrap:
  container_name: "bootstrap"
  image: gdcc/configbaker:latest
  restart: "no"
  environment:
    - TIMEOUT=3m
  command:
    - bootstrap.sh
    - dev
    #- demo
  #volumes:
  #  - ./demo:/scripts/bootstrap/demo
  networks:
    - dataverse
```

- comment out `- dev` and uncomment `#- demo`
- uncomment the two `volumes` lines
- create a `demo/` directory and copy the upstream `init.sh` into it
- **change the unblock key** inside `init.sh` from `unblockme` to something
  private (it gates the admin API after bootstrap)

Then start fresh (delete `data`, `docker compose up`).

> **Trade-off that matters for step 03.** The `demo` persona blocks the admin
> API. The EDMB block is loaded via `POST /api/admin/datasetfield/load`, which
> is an admin endpoint — so with the `demo` persona you must append
> `?unblock-key=YOURKEY` to admin calls. The default `dev` persona needs no key.
> The setup script in step 03 uses the no-key form; if you're on `demo`, see the
> note there.

## Smoke test

Before adding EDMB, confirm the basics work: log in, publish the root
collection, create a collection, create a dataset, upload a file, publish it. If
all of that works, proceed.

